        "bold": true
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "x": 230,
        "y": 178,
        "text": "she has",
        "size": 16,
        "color": "ink",
        "anchor": "start"
       }
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Négation et question",
     "md": "- Négation : **don't** + verbe (I, you, we, they) ; **doesn't** + verbe (he, she, it). She **doesn't play** football.\n- Question : **Do** you play ? / **Does** she play ? (le verbe n'a plus de s).\n- Réponses : **Yes, she does.** / **No, I don't.**"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Exemple 1 : conjuguer",
     "statement": "Écris à la 3e personne : « Amina (study) at school. She (watch) TV in the evening. »",
     "steps": [
      {
       "md": "study : consonne + y → studies."
      },
      {
       "md": "watch : finit en ch → watches."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "**studies** ; **watches**"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Exemple 2 : négation et question",
     "statement": "Transforme « Boris plays football. »",
     "steps": [
      {
       "md": "Négation : doesn't + play → Boris doesn't play football."
      },
      {
       "md": "Question : Does + Boris + play → Does Boris play football ?"
      }
     ],
     "answer": "**Boris doesn't play football.** ; **Does Boris play football?**"
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Erreurs fréquentes",
     "md": "- « He don't like » : **He doesn't like**.\n- « Does she plays ? » : après does, le verbe est **sans s**.\n- Oublier le -s : « She go to school » → **She goes** to school."
    }
   ],
   "reviewNotes": [
    "Les adverbes de fréquence détaillés sont repris en 5e ; ici seulement every day, on Mondays, usually, always, never."
   ]
  },
